            <ArticleTitle>Severe remifentanil-induced acute opioid tolerance following awake craniotomy in an adolescent.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>124-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jclinane.2008.06.032</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Anesthetic goals for procedures involving resections close to the motor cortex include immobility, analgesia, and a level of consciousness that allows for the ability to follow motor commands. Remifentanil as a single agent is an attractive choice, particularly when ventilation is controlled. The successful use of large-dose remifentanil infusion during an awake craniotomy in a 16 year-old man and the subsequent severe acute opioid tolerance is presented.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
